{
  "teamId": 113,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Cincinnati Reds were a team that flirted with greatness but could never quite close the deal. Armed with a powerful offense — 222 home runs, a .759 OPS — and a rotation capable of dominance, they looked like genuine playoff contenders in the spring. But a brutal April (12-14) exposed the volatility that would define their entire season: for every Jesse Winker walk-off hit or Kyle Farmer clutch blast, there was a bullpen implosion waiting in the wings.\n\nThe Reds found their footing through June and July, posting winning records in both months and riding the hot bats of Nick Castellanos, Jonathan India, and Jesse Winker. The highlight of the season came on June 28th, when Castellanos launched a grand slam that embodied everything this lineup was capable of. That 16-10 July record was the closest Cincinnati came to looking like a sure postseason team, and the roster buzzed with the energy of a group that believed it could make a run.\n\nBut the bullpen — Lucas Sims, Heath Hembree, Amir Garrett, Josh Osich — kept undercutting the offense at the worst possible moments. High-leverage situations repeatedly turned into nightmares, with the single most damaging play of the season being a three-run Ozzie Albies homer off Sims in August that carried a -0.813 WPA swing. The relievers' collective volatility meant that even when the Reds scored plenty of runs, they could never build a comfortable cushion in the standings.\n\nSeptember proved fatal. An 11-15 record in the final full month, punctuated by late-game collapses against Washington and others, extinguished any remaining playoff hope. The Reds finished 83-79 — a winning record, a moral victory of sorts, but three games short of the postseason. Nick Castellanos's 31st home run on September 25th felt more like an epitaph than a spark, a reminder of how good this team could be and how much the bullpen had cost them.",
  "chapters": [
